# Cloudflare Pages Redirects file — edge-level 301s evaluated before
# the trailing-slash 308 layer, so a single rule per path catches the
# request directly (no double-hop).

# /security is a common UX convention (GitHub, Stripe, Cloudflare all
# expose it) but RFC 9116 standardizes the endpoint at
# /.well-known/security.txt. Forward both shapes to the canonical one
# so manual /security probes don't 404.
/security   /.well-known/security.txt  301
/security/  /.well-known/security.txt  301

# Legacy slugs and search-intent aliases. These lived in astro.config.mjs's
# `redirects` map until 2026-09-21, where Astro rendered each one as an HTML
# page carrying a meta-refresh. That shape cost two hops, not one: the
# slashless request first took a 308 from `trailingSlash: 'always'`, and only
# the resulting `/alias/` page fired the refresh. Measured on all thirteen.
#
# Cloudflare Pages applies this file "regardless of whether or not an asset
# matches the incoming request", so an edge rule lands ahead of both the
# trailing-slash layer and the asset lookup — one 301, no HTML, no refresh.
# Both shapes of each source are listed because a rule matches one exact path.

# Renamed pages.
/docs/advanced/scenes-and-presets    /docs/advanced/scenes/          301
/docs/advanced/scenes-and-presets/   /docs/advanced/scenes/          301
/docs/usb-leds/adalight-protocol     /docs/usb-leds/serial-protocol/ 301
/docs/usb-leds/adalight-protocol/    /docs/usb-leds/serial-protocol/ 301

